'''Wellington North''' will be a [[New Zealand electorates|New Zealand electorate]], returning a single member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. A former electorate from 1905 to 1946, it has been recreated with new boundaries for the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]].

==Population centres==
The seat covers the central and northern suburbs of [[Wellington]] including the [[Wellington Central|CBD]], [[Thorndon, New Zealand|Thorndon]], [[Pipitea, New Zealand|Pipitea]], [[Te Aro]], [[Aro Valley]], [[Oriental Bay]], [[Mount Victoria (Wellington suburb)|Mount Victoria]], [[Kelburn, New Zealand|Kelburn]], [[Northland, Wellington|Northland]], [[Karori]], [[Wilton, New Zealand|Wilton]], [[Ngaio, New Zealand|Ngaio]], [[Khandallah]], [[Broadmeadows, New Zealand|Broadmeadows]], [[Kaiwharawhara]], [[Crofton Downs]] and [[Wadestown, New Zealand|Wadestown]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Natanahira |first=Tuwhenuaroa |last2=Online |first2=Māori news journalist in Parliament RNZ |date=2025-08-08 |title=From new names to new boundaries: Here's what's happening to your voting electorate |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/569410/from-new-names-to-new-boundaries-here-s-what-s-happening-to-your-voting-electorate |access-date=2025-08-22 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref>

==History==

=== 1905 to 1946 ===
From 1890 until 1905, the four main centres of New Zealand (Auckland, Wellington, Christchurch and Dunedin) were represented by three-member electorates. The City Single Electorates Act 1903 divided those electorates, effective from the end of the [[15th New Zealand Parliament|15th Parliament]]. The City of Wellington electorate was split into the {{NZ electorate link|Wellington East}}, {{NZ electorate link|Wellington Central}}, and Wellington North electorates.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=66ff}}

At its establishment, Wellington North covered areas north of the [[Wellington Central, Wellington|central city]] including the modern suburbs of [[Thorndon, New Zealand|Thorndon]], [[Pipitea, New Zealand|Pipitea]], [[Wadestown, New Zealand|Wadestown]], [[Northland, Wellington|Northland]], [[Kelburn, New Zealand|Kelburn]], [[Te Aro]], [[Wilton, New Zealand|Wilton]] and [[Kaiwharawhara]], as well as [[Matiu / Somes Island|Somes Island]].<ref>{{Cite journal |date=10 April 1905 |title=Supplement to the ''New Zealand Gazette'' of Thursday, April 6, 1905 |url=https://library.victoria.ac.nz/databases/nzgazettearchive/pubs/gazettes/1905/1905%20ISSUE%20033.pdf |journal=[[New Zealand Gazette]] |issue=33 |pages=885–887}}</ref> For the purpose of the [[country quota]], the electorate was always regarded as fully urban.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=71}} In the boundary review before the 1946 general election, Wellington North was one of 26 electorates to be abolished. Suburbs in Wellington North were redistributed between [[Wellington Central (electorate)|Wellington Central]], [[Karori (electorate)|Karori]] and [[Onslow (electorate)|Onslow]].

=== From 2026 ===
The 2025 boundary review required a reduction in North Island electorates by one to reflect nationwide population shifts. The three Wellington City electorates ([[Rongotai (electorate)|Rongotai]], [[Wellington Central (electorate)|Wellington Central]] and [[Ōhāriu (electorate)|Ōhāriu]]) were all underpopulated. All were abolished. The suburbs of [[Ngaio, New Zealand|Ngaio]], [[Khandallah]], [[Broadmeadows, New Zealand|Broadmeadows]], [[Kaiwharawhara]], [[Crofton Downs]] and [[Wadestown, New Zealand|Wadestown]] were combined with most of the former [[Wellington Central (electorate)|Wellington Central electorate]] (including [[Thorndon, New Zealand|Thorndon]], [[Wellington Central|Wellington CBD]], [[Aro Valley]], [[Kelburn, New Zealand|Kelburn]] and [[Mount Victoria (Wellington suburb)|Mount Victoria]]) and the new electorate was named Wellington North.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Natanahira |first=Tuwhenuaroa |last2=Online |first2=Māori news journalist in Parliament RNZ |date=2025-08-08 |title=From new names to new boundaries: Here's what's happening to your voting electorate |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/569410/from-new-names-to-new-boundaries-here-s-what-s-happening-to-your-voting-electorate |access-date=2025-08-22 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=16 September 2025 |page=18 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

===Members of Parliament===
Until its abolition in 1946, the electorate was represented by four members of parliament.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=276}}

[[Charlie Izard]] of the [[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal Party]] was the electorate's first representative from 1905 to {{NZ election link year|1908}},{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=207}} when he was defeated by [[Alexander Herdman]].{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=204, 207}} Herdman had previously represented the {{NZ electorate link|Mount Ida}} electorate. He joined the [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform Party]] but resigned from Parliament in February 1918.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=204}}

Herdman was succeeded by [[John Luke (New Zealand politician)|John Luke]] of the Reform Party in the resulting {{By-election link|Wellington North|1918}}.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=213}} In the {{NZ election link|1928}}, Luke was defeated by [[Charles Chapman (New Zealand politician)|Charles Chapman]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. Chapman represented the electorate from 1928 to 1946, and moved to the {{NZ electorate link|Wellington Central}} electorate when Wellington North was abolished.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=189}}

